  "doctrinal": "Teacher-only: I read 1:18–25 as a real virginal conception, not a pious cover story, and I read 1:22–23 as Matthew meaning what he says — this birth fulfills Isaiah 7:14, not merely rhymes with it. That does not require pretending Isaiah 7 had no meaning in Ahaz's lifetime; Scripture often carries a near word and a farther word without the near one cancelling the far. I would not let the room get stuck in the almah/parthenos lexical fight until they have heard the claim the chapter is actually making: this child is 'God with us,' and he is in David's line by Joseph's legal fatherhood even though Joseph is not the begetter. On the later 'until' debate (perpetual virginity of Mary vs. later children of Joseph and Mary), name both chairs if it comes up, then come back to 1:25's actual job in this chapter — protecting the conception, not writing Mary's lifelong biography.",
