    "2:19–21 — Herod dies. Another angel, another dream, another command to return to 'the land of Israel.' Joseph goes. The geography is covenant geography — not 'back to the house' but back to the land. Matthew keeps using that phrase on purpose.",
    "2:22–23 — Archelaus reigns in Judea in his father's place, and Joseph is afraid. A further warning in a dream turns the family to Galilee, to a town called Nazareth. Fourth fulfillment of the chapter, and the most argued: 'that it might be fulfilled which was spoken by the prophets, He shall be called a Nazarene.' Notice the plural — 'prophets,' not 'the prophet' — and notice that no single Old Testament verse says those words. Matthew knows that. He is gathering a theme, not lifting one line. How that theme is named is a real dispute. What is not in dispute in the text: the King of 2:2 grows up in a town with no prestige, and that too is inside the plan."
