  "points": [
    "3:1–2 — 'In those days came John the Baptist, preaching in the wilderness of Judaea, and saying, Repent ye: for the kingdom of heaven is at hand.' Matthew's first spoken sermon in the book is not Jesus'. It is John's, and it is already the same sentence Jesus will pick up word for word in 4:17. 'Kingdom of heaven' is Matthew's habitual wording for what Mark and Luke usually call the kingdom of God — not a different realm, a Jewish reverence-habit that keeps 'heaven' standing in for the Name. 'At hand' (ēngiken) means it has drawn near. How near, and in what sense it arrives, is a question the rest of the Gospel answers slowly. Do not finish it in verse 2.",
    "3:3 — Matthew's fulfillment move again, this time naming Isaiah 40:3: 'For this is he that was spoken of by the prophet Esaias, saying, The voice of one crying in the wilderness, Prepare ye the way of the Lord, make his paths straight.' In Isaiah that voice prepares a highway for Yahweh himself to lead the exiles home. Matthew puts that voice on John, which means the one coming after John is being identified with the Lord whose way was to be prepared. The citation is Christology, not scenery.",
    "3:4–6 — Camel hair, leather belt, locusts and wild honey. Matthew is dressing John like Elijah in 2 Kings 1:8 on purpose — Malachi 4:5 promised Elijah before the great day, and Jesus will later say John is that Elijah 'if ye will receive it' (11:14; 17:10–13). Jerusalem, Judea, and the Jordan district come out to him and are baptized, 'confessing their sins.' A baptism in the Jordan for Jews is already a hard word: the river that once meant entry into the land is now the place you go to admit you need to come home again.",
    "3:7–10 — Pharisees and Sadducees coming to the baptism get no courtesy paragraph. 'O generation of vipers, who hath warned you to flee from the wrath to come?' Fruit is demanded. Abraham-descent is refused as a shield: 'God is able of these stones to raise up children unto Abraham.' The axe is already at the root. John is not a mascot for later church branding. He is a prophet telling covenant people that the family name will not save them.",
    "3:11–12 — Three baptisms in two verses: John's water, the Coming One's Holy Spirit, and fire. The winnowing fork, the threshing floor, the wheat into the barn, the chaff into unquenchable fire. John does not know the timetable of all that. He knows the Coming One is mightier, and that judgment and gathering arrive with him. Isaiah 4:4 and 44:3 had already paired washing, spirit, and fire around the remnant. John is standing in that stream.",
    "3:13–15 — Jesus comes from Galilee to John to be baptized. John forbids him: 'I have need to be baptized of thee.' Jesus answers with the first words he speaks in this Gospel: 'Suffer it to be so now: for thus it becometh us to fulfil all righteousness.' 'Fulfil' is the same family of language as the formula in chapters 1–2. 'Righteousness' is the same family as Joseph the just man in 1:19. Jesus is not confessing his own sin in the river. He is stepping into the queue of the sinful people he came to save, and calling that act righteousness fulfilled. How exactly that works is a live question. That he insists on it is not.",
    "3:16–17 — He comes up from the water. The heavens open. The Spirit of God descends like a dove and comes on him. A voice from heaven: 'This is my beloved Son, in whom I am well pleased.' Psalm 2:7 ('Thou art my Son') and Isaiah 42:1 ('mine elect, in whom my soul delighteth') are standing under that sentence. The baptism is not only about water. It is the public identification of the Son by the Father and the Spirit — Matthew's first clear triune scene — immediately before the Spirit drives that same Son into the wilderness in 4:1."
  ],
  "refs": [
    "Isaiah 40:3–5 — the wilderness voice that prepares Yahweh's own coming. Matthew 3:3 puts that voice on John, which pulls the weight of 'the Lord' onto Jesus.",
    "2 Kings 1:8 — Elijah's hair-garment and leather belt. Matthew 3:4 is costume-as-theology.",
    "Malachi 3:1 and 4:5–6 — the messenger, and Elijah sent before the day of the Lord. Matthew will return to this explicitly in 11:10, 14 and 17:10–13.",
    "Psalm 2:7 — 'Thou art my Son; this day have I begotten thee.' The heavenly voice in 3:17 is speaking royal adoption language over a wet man in the Jordan.",
    "Isaiah 42:1–4 — the Servant the Lord delights in, the one who receives the Spirit and will not snap a bruised reed. 'In whom I am well pleased' is that Servant song begun.",
    "Isaiah 11:1–2 — the Branch (netzer again) on whom the Spirit of the Lord rests. Chapter 2's Nazareth question and chapter 3's dove belong on the same table.",
    "Matthew 4:17 — Jesus' first sermon is John's sentence reused. The kingdom announcement does not change speakers by changing content."
  ],
  "hist": [
    "The wilderness of Judea and the Jordan Rift were already loaded with memory: forty years, entry into the land, Elijah disappearing across this same river (2 Kings 2). A prophet standing there in Elijah's clothes, washing Jews as if they were incoming Gentiles, was a political and theological act. Herod Antipas will kill John later for a different offense (14:1–12). The threat starts here.",
    "Pharisees and Sadducees rarely stand in the same sentence as allies. Pharisees were the lay holiness movement; Sadducees held the Temple aristocracy and denied resurrection (22:23). John lining them up together as vipers is saying the axe is at both roots — popular religion and official religion.",
    "Josephus describes John as a good man who called Jews to virtue and baptism, and says crowds gathered in a way that frightened Herod Antipas (Antiquities 18.116–119). That outside notice does not give us Matthew's theology, but it does mean John was not an invented stage-prop."
  ],
