  "doctrinal": "Teacher-only: I read 3:16–17 as a real disclosure of Father, Son, and Spirit at the Jordan — not later dogma glued onto a simple dunking. I read 3:15 as Jesus identifying with the people he will redeem, not as Jesus admitting personal guilt. That puts me closest to the 'solidarity / true Israel' chair below. I would say that as mine. I would not let a group walk out thinking baptism is how Jesus became the Son; the voice announces what he already is. And I would not let John's fire-language get translated into a vague inner warmth. John is talking about judgment and harvest arriving with the King.",
  "story": {
    "scene": "The Jordan in ordinary weather. A crowd that smells like travel and confession. A prophet who looks like he has been living off the land because he has.\n\nThen a man John knows — cousin, greater one, the one whose sandal he is not worthy to carry — stepping into the water as if he belonged in this line.\n\nJohn tries to stop it. Jesus will not be stopped. He goes under the same river as the sinners of Jerusalem. He comes up. The sky, which has been brass for four hundred years of prophetic silence in Israel's memory, tears.",
    "modern": "The holy person in the room refuses the platform and takes the chair in the back. Not as a stunt. As obedience.\n\nSomeone who does not need the confession line stands in it anyway, because the people he loves are in it. That is the shock of 3:13–15. If your modern parallel turns into 'Jesus needed to get right with God,' you have told the wrong story."
  },
