  "chNum": "4",
  "points": [
    "4:1–2 — 'Then was Jesus led up of the Spirit into the wilderness to be tempted of the devil.' The same Spirit who just descended like a dove in 3:16 is the one who leads him toward the adversary. Forty days and forty nights of fasting — Moses on Sinai (Exodus 34:28) and Elijah on the way to Horeb (1 Kings 19:8) and Israel’s forty years all standing behind the number. Matthew has already called Jesus out of Egypt as God's Son (2:15). Now the Son walks the wilderness road Israel walked, hungry on purpose.",
    "4:3–4 — First test, aimed at the baptismal voice: 'If thou be the Son of God, command that these stones be made bread.' The devil is not wondering whether the voice told the truth. He is probing what kind of Son Jesus will be — a Son who uses the office to feed himself, or a Son who waits. Jesus answers from Deuteronomy 8:3, the manna chapter: 'Man shall not live by bread alone, but by every word that proceedeth out of the mouth of God.' Israel craved bread and forgot the word. The true Son quotes the word and stays hungry.",
    "4:5–7 — Second test: the holy city, the pinnacle of the Temple, and a Scripture quoted back at him. The devil cites Psalm 91:11–12 — angels will bear you up — and leaves out Psalm 91:13, the part about treading on the serpent. Jesus answers from Deuteronomy 6:16: 'Thou shalt not tempt the Lord thy God,' the verse that remembers Massah, where Israel demanded that God prove himself on command (Exodus 17:1–7). A Son who throws himself off the Temple to force a public rescue would be staging faith, not offering it.",
    "4:8–10 — Third test: 'an exceeding high mountain,' all the kingdoms of the world and their glory, offered for one act of worship. Whether Matthew means a bodily transport or a visionary showing, the offer is the old one from the garden and from the golden calf — glory without the cross. Jesus answers from Deuteronomy 6:13: 'Thou shalt worship the Lord thy God, and him only shalt thou serve.' Three tests, three answers, all from Israel's wilderness book. Matthew's throughline is not subtle: where the first son (Israel) failed in the wilderness, this Son recites the same book and does it.",
    "4:11 — The devil leaves. Angels come and minister. Luke 4:13 will add 'for a season.' Matthew simply ends the scene and moves the Son into public ministry. The victory is real. It is not the last approach.",
    "4:12–16 — John is cast into prison; Jesus withdraws into Galilee and leaves Nazareth for Capernaum by the sea, in Zebulun and Naphtali. Then the fulfillment formula again: 'that it might be fulfilled which was spoken by Esaias the prophet.' Matthew quotes Isaiah 9:1–2 — the land that walked in darkness, Galilee of the Gentiles, a great light. This is the same Isaiah chapter that names a child called Wonderful, Counsellor, the Prince of Peace, on David's throne (Isaiah 9:6–7). Matthew stops the quote at the light dawning. He expects anyone who knows Isaiah 9 to hear the rest. The ministry does not start in Jerusalem. It starts in the north the elites could ignore, on Gentile-shadowed ground.",
    "4:17 — 'From that time Jesus began to preach, and to say, Repent: for the kingdom of heaven is at hand.' Matthew marks a hinge with 'from that time' (he will do it again at 16:21). The first public words of Jesus in this Gospel are John's words. The King is now the preacher of his own nearness.",
    "4:18–22 — Two calls, four names: Peter and Andrew casting a net; James and John mending a net with Zebedee. 'Follow me, and I will make you fishers of men.' They leave nets, boat, and father. Jeremiah 16:16 had promised fishers sent to find scattered Israel. Jesus uses the trade in front of him as the new mission-image. The speed of their leaving is meant to be felt. Matthew does not pause to explain their psychology.",
    "4:23–25 — A summary that will govern chapters 5–9: teaching in synagogues, preaching the gospel of the kingdom, healing every disease. Galilee, Decapolis, Jerusalem, Judea, and beyond Jordan all send people. Before the Sermon on the Mount begins, Matthew wants the crowd already around him — so that 5:1 is not a private seminar but a public mountain."
  ],
